[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a complete, copy-and-paste-ready recipe for loading a **single `.pmtiles` archive** (vector, raster or raster-DEM) into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles helper library

MapLibre itself does not understand the `pmtiles://` protocol, but the **`pmtiles` npm package** ships a tiny protocol handler that does the range-request magic for you.

```bash
# From your project root
npm install pmtiles   # or yarn add pmtiles
```

> **Tip:** The current `pmtiles` package (≥ 3.0.0) works with every MapLibre version (v3-v6) because it ships a compatibility shim that adapts to the new `addProtocol` signature.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** (before any map is created)

```js
// src/pmtiles-setup.js  (or any module that runs at app start)
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Create the protocol instance
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it under the name "pmtiles"
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);

// (Optional) clean-up when the last map is destroyed
export const removePmtilesProtocol = () => {
  maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
};
```

> **Why register only once?**  
> `addProtocol` adds a global handler. Registering it inside a React component’s render loop, for example, would add the same handler many times and can cause memory-leaks or duplicate requests.

---

## 3️⃣ Reference the archive in a **style source**

You have three possible source types:

| Type | When to use | Example `type` value |
|------|-------------|----------------------|
| Vector tiles | Streets, boundaries, POIs, etc. | `"vector"` |
| Raster imagery | Satellite, orthophoto, etc. | `"raster"` |
| Raster-DEM (elevation) | Terrain, hillshade, 3-D terrain | `"raster-dem"` (plus `encoding`) |

### 3.1 Vector-tile source (most common)

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // <-- the PMTiles source
      myPmtiles: {
        type: 'vector',
        // The protocol handler reads the archive header and returns a TileJSON,
        // so you **must use `url:`**, not `tiles:` or `tileset:`.
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Example: draw water from the "water" layer inside the archive
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'water',          // exact layer name inside the PMTiles file
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },
      // Example: draw roads from the "road" layer
      {
        id: 'roads',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#777',
          'line-width': 1.5
        }
      }
      // …add as many layers as you need, each with its own `source-layer`
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

### 3.2 Raster-imagery source

```js
{
  id: 'satellite',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'myPmtiles',
  'source-layer': 'imagery',   // raster layers usually have a single layer name
  paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.9 }
}
```

### 3.3 Raster-DEM (terrain) source

```js
{
  // First, declare the DEM source
  id: 'terrain',
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/elevation.pmtiles',
  // The encoding tells MapLibre how to decode the elevation values.
  // Most PMTiles DEMs use "terrarium" (or "mapbox").
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // Optional: set maxzoom if you want to limit over-zooming.
  maxzoom: 14
}

// Then enable terrain in the map options (outside the style object)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: myStyleObject,
  terrain: { source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 }   // <-- hook the DEM source
});
```

---

## 4️⃣ Make sure the host can serve **HTTP Range** requests & CORS

| Host | What you need to check |
|------|------------------------|
| **AWS S3** | `Accept-Ranges: bytes` is automatic. Add `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000` and `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |
| **Cloudflare R2** | Same as S3 – it’s S3-compatible. |
| **GitHub Pages** | Files ≤ 100 MiB are allowed. Add a `CORS` header via a `.github/workflows` step or a `static` site config. |
| **Netlify / Vercel** | Both support range requests; just enable CORS in the site settings or via a `_headers` file (`/*  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`). |
| **Any CDN** | Verify that `Range` and `Accept-Ranges` are passed through unchanged. |

If the server does **not** return `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` (or blocks the `Range` header), the browser will abort the tile fetch and you’ll see errors like:

```
Failed to load resource: the server responded with a status of 403 (Forbidden)
```

---

## 5️⃣ Verify the archive (optional but recommended)

```bash
# Install the binary (once)
curl -L -o pmtiles https://github.com/protomaps/go-pmtiles/releases/download/v2.5.0/pmtiles-linux-amd64
chmod +x pmtiles

# Show header & metadata
./pmtiles show world.pmtiles
# Verify integrity
./pmtiles verify world.pmtiles
```

If the header reports the correct `minzoom`, `maxzoom`, and `bounds`, you can be confident the style will pick up the right zoom range automatically.

---

## 6️⃣ Full Minimal Example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.2.0/+esm';

  // ---- 1️⃣ Register protocol -------------------------------------------------
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // ---- 2️⃣ Create the map ----------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        world: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/world.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: {
            'line-color': '#777',
            'line-width': 1.2
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser (or serve it via a local static server) and you should see the vector data coming straight from the `.pmtiles` archive.

---

## 7️⃣ Common Pitfalls & How to Fix Them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Blank tiles / no data** | Used `tiles: ['pmtiles://…/{z}/{x}/{y}']` instead of `url:` | Switch to `url:` – the protocol reads the archive header and supplies the correct TileJSON. |
| **`Error: Tile fetch error: 404`** | Wrong URL or the host blocks `Range` requests | Verify the file is publicly reachable and that `Accept-Ranges: bytes` is present. |
| **CORS error** | Host missing `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` | Add the header (or configure the static host to send it). |
| **MapLibre v4+ throws “handler must resolve to {data}”** | Using an old `pmtiles` version (< 3) that still uses callbacks | Upgrade `pmtiles` to ≥ 3.0.0 (the `Protocol().tile` shim now returns `{data}`). |
| **Zoom levels beyond the archive are missing** | You referenced the source with `tiles:` instead of `url:` (MapLibre assumes `maxzoom: 22`) | Use `url:`; the protocol will automatically set `maxzoom` from the archive header, and MapLibre will over-zoom gracefully. |
| **Large file (> 100 MiB) on GitHub Pages fails** | GitHub Pages limits static files to 100 MiB | Host the file on S3, R2, Cloudflare R2, or a CDN instead. |

---

## 8️⃣ Quick Checklist Before Going Live

1. **PMTiles file** – generated, `pmtiles show` reports correct bounds/zoom.
2. **Host** – serves the file with `Accept-Ranges: bytes` and proper CORS.
3. **Protocol registration** – done once, before any `new maplibregl.Map()`.
4. **Style source** – uses `type` (`vector`/`raster`/`raster-dem`) and **`url:`** with the `pmtiles://` prefix.
5. **Layer definitions** – correct `source-layer` names (inspect with `pmtiles show` or a viewer like `pmtiles-cli tile`).
6. **Cache headers** – long-term (`max-age=31536000`) if the archive never changes.
7. **Test** – open the page, open DevTools → Network, confirm that requests are `Range: bytes=…` and that responses have `206 Partial Content`.

