```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// 1️⃣ Register protocol (once)
const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

// 2️⃣ Build the map
new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      myData: {
        type: 'vector',
        url: 'pmtiles://https://cdn.example.com/mydata.pmtiles'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      {
        id: 'land',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'myData',
        'source-layer': 'land',
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#eaeaea' }
      }
      // …more layers
    ]
  },
  center: [-122.4, 37.8],
  zoom: 12
});
```

That’s it—your `.pmtiles` archive is now a first-class tile source, served from any static host, with zero server-side code required. Happy mapping! 🚀

### What changed in MapLibre GL JS v4?

`maplibregl.addProtocol()` used to accept a **callback-style** handler:

```js
// v3 (and earlier)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
});
```

Starting with **v4** the API became **promise-based** and the handler must return an **object that contains a `data` property** (the raw tile bytes).  
The second argument is now an **`AbortController`** (instead of a callback) so the old signature is ignored – the library thinks the handler succeeded but receives no `data`, which results in blank tiles.

> **Result of the old code on v4** – the request succeeds, but MapLibre reads `undefined` for `data` → empty tile.

---

## How to fix it

You have three options:

1. **Rewrite the handler to the new signature** (recommended for any custom source).  
2. **Wrap the old callback-style code in a tiny shim** that converts it to the new contract.  
3. **If you are only using PMTiles**, let the `pmtiles` library do the work for you – its `protocol.tile` method already implements the v4 shim.

Below are concrete examples for each approach.

---

### 1️⃣ Rewrite the handler (pure v4 style)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Register a custom protocol called "mytiles"
maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abortController) => {
  // `params.url` is the full URL that MapLibre wants to fetch.
  // The AbortController lets the request be cancelled if the map no longer needs the tile.
  const response = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });

  if (!response.ok) {
    // Throwing aborts the request and makes MapLibre treat it as a tile error.
    throw new Error(`Tile fetch failed: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
  }

  // Get the raw bytes (ArrayBuffer, Uint8Array, Blob, or Response are all OK)
  const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // IMPORTANT: wrap the bytes in an object with a `data` key.
  // You may also return `cacheControl` and `expires` if you want to control HTTP caching.
  return { data: buffer };
});
```

**Key points**

| What the new API expects | Why it matters |
|--------------------------|----------------|
| `async (params, abortController)` | Allows you to `await` fetches and respect cancellation. |
| Return **`{ data: <bytes> }`** | MapLibre extracts the tile from the `data` property. A bare `ArrayBuffer` is ignored. |
| Throw an `Error` on failure | Propagates the error to the map (red-cross tile, console warning). |
| Optional `cacheControl` / `expires` | Lets you influence the browser/ CDN cache for that tile. |

---

### 2️⃣ Tiny shim that keeps your old callback code

If you have a lot of existing callback-style handlers and don’t want to touch them all, wrap them:

```js
function callbackToPromiseHandler(cbHandler) {
  // Returns a new handler that matches the v4 contract
  return async (params, abortController) => {
    // Return a promise that resolves when the callback is called
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      // The old handler expects (params, callback)
      cbHandler(params, (err, result) => {
        if (err) return reject(err);
        // `result` is whatever the old code passed (ArrayBuffer, Blob, etc.)
        resolve({ data: result });
      });
    });
  };
}

// Example: your old callback-style function
function oldHandler(params, callback) {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(e => callback(e));
}

// Register the shimmed version
maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', callbackToPromiseHandler(oldHandler));
```

Now the old code runs unchanged, but the wrapper converts the callback result into the `{data}` object that v4 expects.

---

### 3️⃣ Let the **pmtiles** library do it for you

If you are serving **PMTiles** (the most common use-case), you don’t need to write any handler at all. The library ships a ready-made, version-agnostic handler that works on v3, v4, v5 and v6.

```bash
npm install pmtiles
```

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// Create the protocol object (it knows how to do range requests, caching, etc.)
const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it under the "pmtiles" scheme
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);
```

That single line replaces any custom callback code and automatically:

* Handles HTTP **Range** requests.
* Returns `{data, cacheControl?, expires?}` in the correct shape.
* Works with both vector and raster PMTiles.
* Supports aborting via the `AbortController`.

> **Note:** The `pmtiles` library’s `protocol.tile` method already contains a **v3-compat shim**. If you are still on `pmtiles` < 3 you must upgrade to at least `3.0.0` because older versions only expose the callback API.

---

## Full minimal example (vector PMTiles)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS v4 + PMTiles</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // Register the PMTiles protocol (once, before any map is created)
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // The URL can be a plain HTTPS link or `pmtiles://` – both work.
        world: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/world.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 1 }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*The only MapLibre-specific line that changed from v3 → v4 is the `addProtocol` registration; the rest of the style stays the same.*

---

## Quick checklist after the upgrade

| ✅ | What to verify |
|---|----------------|
| **Protocol registration** | `maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abort) => …)` (or `pmtiles.Protocol().tile`). |
| **Return shape** | Must resolve to an object with a `data` property (`{data: Uint8Array|ArrayBuffer|Blob|Response}`). |
| **Abort handling** | Pass `signal: abortController.signal` to `fetch` (or any async work) so cancelled tiles don’t waste bandwidth. |
| **CORS & Range** | The host serving the tiles must return `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain) **and** `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. |
| **Cache headers** | Long-term `Cache-Control` on the `.pmtiles` file (e.g. `public, max-age=31536000`). |
| **PMTiles version** | Use `pmtiles` ≥ 3.0.0 (the v3-compat shim is built-in). |
| **Remove old callback code** | Delete any `addProtocol(..., (params, cb) => …)` that still exists; replace with one of the three approaches above. |

