## TL;DR code snippet you can copy-paste

```js
// ---- If you have your own custom tile source ----
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abort) => {
  const resp = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abort.signal });
  if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`Tile error ${resp.status}`);
  const buf = await resp.arrayBuffer();
  return { data: buf };               // <-- must be wrapped!
});

// ---- If you are using PMTiles (recommended) ----
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);
```

That’s all you need to get your custom tiles (or PMTiles) working again on MapLibre GL JS v4 (and future v5/v6). Happy mapping!

**Short answer:** No – PMTiles is a *static* archive. It’s perfect for basemaps, historic layers, or any dataset that doesn’t change after the file is written, but it can’t be updated every 30 seconds from a live PostGIS table.  

Below is a quick decision matrix, a short explanation of why PMTiles won’t work for live bus positions, and a few practical patterns you can use instead (you can still keep a PMTiles-based basemap if you like).

---

## Decision matrix

| Requirement | Best fit |
|-------------|----------|
| **Static, pre-generated tiles** (basemap, land-use, terrain) | **PMTiles** (single-file, CDN-friendly) |
| **Dynamic data that changes frequently** (bus locations, traffic, live sensor feeds) | **Tile server / vector-tile endpoint** **or** **GeoJSON/FeatureCollection fetch** (or WebSocket) |
| **Hybrid** – static basemap + live overlay | Use **PMTiles for the basemap** + a **dynamic source** for the bus layer |

---

## Why PMTiles isn’t suitable for live bus positions

| PMTiles characteristic | What it means for a live feed |
|------------------------|--------------------------------|
| **File-based archive** – the whole pyramid (all zoom levels) is written once and never changes. | You would have to rewrite the entire `.pmtiles` file every 30 s, upload it, and wait for the CDN to propagate – impossible for real-time use. |
| **Range-request only** – the client asks for byte ranges that already exist in the file. | No way to ask “give me the latest bus point at this location” because that point isn’t in the file yet. |
| **Cache-friendly, immutable** – you set a long `Cache-Control` header. | Real-time data needs a short-lived cache (or no cache) so the map sees the newest positions. |
| **No server-side logic** – the host just serves bytes. | You need a server that can run a SQL query (`SELECT * FROM bus_positions WHERE …`) on every request. |

In short: PMTiles is a *read-only* container. It’s great for “once-generated” data, not for “update every 30 s”.

---

## Recommended patterns for live bus positions

### 1. **Vector-tile endpoint (recommended for many points)**
- **How it works:** A small tile server (e.g. [Martin](https://github.com/maplibre/martin), [tileserver-gl](https://github.com/maptiler/tileserver-gl), or a custom Node/Go/Java service) reads the PostGIS table on-the-fly and returns MapLibre-compatible vector tiles (`application/x-protobuf`).
- **Pros:**  
  - Scales well when you have thousands of buses.  
  - Tiles are automatically clipped to the viewport/zoom, reducing bandwidth.  
  - You can still use a static PMTiles basemap for the background.
- **Cons:** Requires a server (but it can be serverless – e.g. AWS Lambda + API Gateway, Cloudflare Workers, or Vercel Functions).

**Example with Martin (Node/TS):**

```ts
import { createServer } from '@maplibre/martin';
import { Pool } from 'pg';

const pg = new Pool({
  connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL,
});

const server = createServer({
  // Tell Martin how to fetch vector tiles from PostGIS
  // It will call this function for each tile request
  async getTile(z, x, y) {
    const sql = `
      SELECT ST_AsMVT(tile, 'bus', 4096, 'geom')
      FROM (
        SELECT ST_AsMVTGeom(
                 ST_Transform(geom, 3857),
                 ST_TileEnvelope($1, $2, $3),
                 4096, 0, true
               ) AS geom,
               id, route, bearing, speed
        FROM bus_positions
        WHERE ST_Intersects(
                ST_Transform(geom, 3857),
                ST_TileEnvelope($1, $2, $3)
              )
      ) AS tile;
    `;
    const { rows } = await pg.query(sql, [z, x, y]);
    return rows[0]?.st_asmvt ?? Buffer.alloc(0);
  },
});

server.listen(3000, () => console.log('Martin listening on :3000'));
```

**MapLibre side:**

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // static basemap (optional)
      basemap: {
        type: 'vector',
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.com/europe-basemap.pmtiles'
      },
      // live bus positions
      buses: {
        type: 'vector',
        // Martin serves TileJSON at /tiles.json
        url: 'https://my-api.example.com/tiles.json?layer=buses'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // basemap layers …
      {
        id: 'bus-points',
        type: 'circle',
        source: 'buses',
        'source-layer': 'bus', // name you used in ST_AsMVT
        paint: {
          'circle-radius': 6,
          'circle-color': '#ff6600',
          'circle-stroke-width': 1,
          'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});
```

*Every map view triggers a tile request (`/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`).* The server runs the SQL query, returns only the buses that intersect that tile, and the map updates instantly when you call `map.triggerRepaint()` or simply let the browser request the same tile again after the cache expires (set `Cache-Control: max-age=0` on the tile endpoint).

---

### 2. **GeoJSON/FeatureCollection polling (good for < ~500 points)**
- **How it works:** Expose a simple HTTP endpoint that returns a GeoJSON `FeatureCollection` of the latest bus positions. Poll it every 30 s (or use `setInterval`) and replace the source data.
- **Pros:** Very easy to implement; no tile server needed. Works fine if the number of buses is modest.
- **Cons:** Bandwidth grows linearly with the number of points; the whole payload is sent on each poll. Not ideal for large fleets or high zoom levels.

**Server (Node/Express example):**

```js
app.get('/bus-positions.geojson', async (req, res) => {
  const { rows } = await pg.query(`
    SELECT id, route, ST_AsGeoJSON(geom) AS geom,
           bearing, speed
    FROM bus_positions;
  `);
  const features = rows.map(r => ({
    type: 'Feature',
    geometry: JSON.parse(r.geom),
    properties: {
      id: r.id,
      route: r.route,
      bearing: r.bearing,
      speed: r.speed
    }
  }));
  res.json({ type: 'FeatureCollection', features });
});
```

**MapLibre side:**

```js
// Create an empty GeoJSON source once
map.addSource('buses', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: { type: 'FeatureCollection', features: [] }
});

// Add a layer that draws the points
map.addLayer({
  id: 'bus-points',
  type: 'circle',
  source: 'buses',
  paint: {
    'circle-radius': 5,
    'circle-color': '#ff6600',
    'circle-stroke-width': 1,
    'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
  }
});

// Poll every 30 s
async function refreshBuses() {
  const resp = await fetch('https://my-api.example.com/bus-positions.geojson');
  const geojson = await resp.json();
  map.getSource('buses').setData(geojson);
}
refreshBuses();               // initial load
setInterval(refreshBuses, 30_000);
```

*Tip:* If you want smoother motion, you can animate the points between polls (e.g., using `requestAnimationFrame` and interpolating lat/lon based on timestamps).

---

### 3. **WebSocket / Server-Sent Events (real-time push)**
- **How it works:** The server pushes each bus update as soon as it arrives (e.g., after a GPS fix). The client updates the GeoJSON source or a custom `custom` source.
- **Pros:** Near-zero latency, no polling waste.
- **Cons:** Slightly more complex infrastructure (WebSocket server, authentication, scaling). Still best for modest fleets; for thousands of points you may want to fall back to tiled queries.

**Client snippet (WebSocket):**

```js
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://my-api.example.com/bus-updates');
ws.addEventListener('message', e => {
  const update = JSON.parse(e.data); // {id, lon, lat, bearing, speed, ...}
  // Keep a map of features by id for quick replace
  const source = map.getSource('buses');
  const fc = source._data; // internal reference; or keep your own copy
  const idx = fc.features.findIndex(f => f.properties.id === update.id);
  const feature = {
    type: 'Feature',
    geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [update.lon, update.lat] },
    properties: { ...update }
  };
  if (idx >= 0) fc.features[idx] = feature;
  else fc.features.push(feature);
  source.setData(fc);
});
```

---

## Hybrid approach (static basemap + live overlay)

You can **still use PMTiles** for the background map (e.g., a country-level basemap, terrain, or satellite imagery) and pair it with any of the dynamic patterns above for the bus layer.

```js
// style.sources.basemap → pmtiles://https://cdn.example.com/europe.pmtiles
// style.sources.buses   → vector-tile endpoint OR geojson source
```

This gives you the performance and CDN benefits of a static basemap while keeping the bus data fresh.

---

## Quick checklist before you decide

| Question | Answer → Recommended approach |
|----------|------------------------------|
| **Do I need sub-second updates?** | Use WebSocket or short-interval polling. |
| **How many moving objects?** | < ~500 → GeoJSON polling is fine. > ~500 → tiled endpoint (Martin, tileserver-gl). |
| **Do I already have a tile server?** | Add a new layer that queries PostGIS on each tile request. |
| **Do I want zero-ops hosting for the basemap?** | Keep the basemap in PMTiles on S3/Cloudflare R2 and serve the live layer separately. |
| **Do I need terrain or raster imagery?** | Those can be PMTiles (`type: 'raster'` or `'raster-dem'`) – they stay static. |

---

## TL;DR code skeleton

```js
// 1️⃣ Register PMTiles (static basemap) – one-time at app start
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
const pmProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmProtocol.tile);

// 2️⃣ Create the map with two sources
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      basemap: {
        type: 'vector',
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.com/europe-basemap.pmtiles'
      },
      // Choose ONE of the three dynamic patterns below:
      // a) vector-tile endpoint (Martin, tileserver-gl)
      buses: { type: 'vector', url: 'https://my-api.example.com/tiles.json' },

      // b) geojson polling
      // buses: { type: 'geojson', data: {type:'FeatureCollection',features:[]} },

      // c) geojson via WebSocket (same source definition as b)
    },
    layers: [
      // …basemap layers…
      {
        id: 'bus-points',
        type: 'circle',
        source: 'buses',
        'source-layer': 'bus', // only for vector-tile endpoint
        paint: {
          'circle-radius': 6,
          'circle-color': '#ff6600',
          'circle-stroke-width': 1,
          'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});

// 3️⃣ If you chose GeoJSON polling, start the interval:
if (map.getSource('buses').type === 'geojson') {
  async function refresh() {
    const resp = await fetch('https://my-api.example.com/bus-positions.geojson');
    const data = await resp.json();
    map.getSource('buses').setData(data);
  }
  refresh();
  setInterval(refresh, 30_000);
}
```

